{"author":{"id":"b409816c5cd5ab140d1d52eb33aace35de44952bb794b19806fd57f6ecb1ace9"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1789380391,"nonce":"nEB+atWoPUlAsOv4AiINuJpkrs4=","title":"Constructors never inlined: an allocating callee was Unsupported","message":"Both inliner classifiers rejected any callee containing a call to an\nintrinsic outside the inline-safe set, so a function holding\n`intrinsic.malloc` or `intrinsic.free` (every `Class$new`, every boxing\nhelper the boxes pass has expanded) stayed a call however small and\nhowever hot. The copy of such a call in the caller means what the\noriginal meant: it is a plain runtime call with side effects, not\nsomething that depends on the enclosing function the way `Alloca`,\n`Await` or `Yield` do.\n\n`is_plain_call_intrinsic` names the intrinsics that may be copied\n(memory, allocation, refcounts, overflow checks, panic and abort); a\ncallee holding one is call-bearing, so it inlines under the same\nbudget and hot-site rule as a callee calling a function. In\n`bench_op_overload.py` the `Vec3$new` in the loop becomes the malloc\nand stores in place: 144 to 125 ms for twenty million additions.\n\nUncovered the OSR live-in width bug (2549b4f) by changing the frame\nshape.","files":null}]}